course: dictionary
lesson: 15887
title: The prefix un- and the word uninterested
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The prefix un- means not. Add it to a word and you often get the opposite: happy becomes unhappy, and kind becomes unkind. So uninterested is interested with un- added, and it means not interested.
explain: Uninterested means not wanting to know about something, or not paying attention to it. «The cat looked uninterested in the toy.» The cat did not want to play with it and did not seem to care. Check by swapping in your meaning: the cat looked not interested in the toy still makes sense.
explain: You can often tell someone is uninterested from how they look or act. «Mia gave a yawn and stared out of the window.» Yawning, looking away and not answering questions are clues that someone is uninterested.
fact: The prefix un- means not, so uninterested means not interested.
fact: «Uninterested» is an adjective, so it describes a person or an animal.
fact: Common phrases are «uninterested in» and «look uninterested».
fact: «Uninterested» means not wanting to know about something or pay attention to it.
fact: Uninterested is about attention, not feelings: it tells you someone is not paying attention to something.
exercise: «Sam was uninterested in the film and kept checking his phone.» What does uninterested mean? | Lost on the way to the cinema | Hungry for popcorn | *Not wanting to watch or pay attention | Very excited about the film
exercise: The word uninterested starts with un-. What does the prefix un- mean? | Again | Very | Before | *Not
exercise: Which sentence best shows someone is uninterested? | Mia rushed to the front to get a good seat | *Mia yawned and looked out of the window during the talk | Mia clapped and asked three questions about the talk | Mia took notes and smiled at the speaker
test: What does the word uninterested mean? | Very angry about something | *Not interested; not paying attention to something | Lost and confused | Keen to know more about something
test: Which word is the opposite of interested? | interpret | *uninterested | interesting | interest
exam: «Ben looked uninterested as the teacher read the story.» Why does the author use uninterested? | To show Ben was in a hurry | *To show Ben was not paying attention | To show Ben was crying | To show Ben had lost his book
